                <pb facs="1482" n="1472" sameAs="frus:frus1945v07/pg1472"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">125.8576/11–2445: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Robertson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Chungking</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1945-11-24T15:00:00+07:00">November 24,
                            1945—3 p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received November 24—10:17 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>2031. Permission install and operate Navy radio station in Embassy at Nanking and
                    also at Consulates where commercial facilities are inadequate was requested of
                    Chinese Govt in our note of Oct. 9 to Foreign Office. (Dept’s 1886, Nov.
                        22<anchor n="37" xml:id="d1133-f1"/>) After repeated inquiries Foreign
                    Office informed us yesterday that matter had been referred to Generalissimo for
                    decision, and that his decision, probably favorable, would be communicated to us
                    within few days. (Sent Dept as 2031, repeated Shanghai.) Upon receipt of
                    decision we will telegraph our recommendations as requested in Shanghai’s 43,
                    Nov. 8 regarding advisability of using OWI or Naval radio network for our
                    offices in China.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Robertson</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
